{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Great Northern's Montana north-south line: Havre on the transcontinental main, south through Great Falls to Helena and Butte. It was built as two companies at once -- the St. Paul, Minneapolis and Manitoba pushing west from Devils Lake to Great Falls (Oct 16 1887), and James J. Hill's Montana Central running on south to Helena (November 1887) and Butte (Nov 10 1888). Both passed to the Great Northern on Feb 1 1890, and the Montana Central was absorbed outright in 1907. The passenger trains that used it connected Havre to Great Falls, Helena and Butte; the last of them, a single self-propelled railcar known as the galloping goose, ran to Butte for the last time on Sep 6 1961. That date is the end of passenger rail at Great Falls on this route. The corridor is still in freight service north of Helena, but freight-only years are not drawn (CLAUDE.md Rule 1).", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montana_Central_Railway (Montana Central incorporated Jan 25 1886 by the St. Paul, Minneapolis and Manitoba; Devils Lake-Fort Assinniboine-Great Falls link complete Oct 16 1887; Helena November 1887; Butte Nov 10 1888; ownership transferred to the Great Northern Feb 1 1890; Montana Central absorbed into the Great Northern in 1907; standard gauge).", "https://storyofbutte.org/items/show/3527 (Great Northern Passenger Station, Butte) (Butte's Great Northern passenger station opened Feb 1 1914 and \"served the railway's passenger trains that connected Havre on the transcontinental line to Great Falls, Helena, and Butte\"; the last service was a single self-propelled railcar, the \"galloping goose\"; \"Service to Butte ended on September 6, 1961\")."], "not_yet_sourced": "Every headway_min and avg_speed_kmh here is an estimate -- no Great Northern public timetable or Official Guide page was consulted. Train names and numbers for this route were not found. The 1950 boundary inside the Great Northern feature is editorial (a decline marker), not a sourced event. No documented mileage was found for Helena-Butte, so that leg's traced length is unvalidated. Whether passenger service on the Havre-Great Falls half ended on the same day as the Butte service is not sourced: Sep 6 1961 is documented for Butte, and is used here for the whole line. Intermediate stations were not compiled, so the route is drawn endpoint-to-endpoint along the corridor rather than station by station.", "geometry_sources": ["Traced on real track in OpenStreetMap by corridor-constrained Dijkstra over every railway=rail/abandoned/disused/razed way in the corridor, between OSM place nodes; simplified Douglas-Peucker at 40 m. No straight-line approximations beyond the sub-kilometre bridges noted per feature.", "Overpass API (maps.mail.ru mirror), bbox dumps of every railway way in the Havre-Great Falls-Helena-Butte corridors, pulled 2026-08-26. Endpoints are OSM place nodes for Havre, Great Falls, Helena and Butte.", "Leg fits: Great Falls-Helena 152.1 km traced vs ~154 km documented (0.99x); Havre-Great Falls 198.4 km vs ~193 km (1.03x); Helena-Butte 116.0 km, no documented figure found."], "pulled": "20260826"}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Montana Central Railway", "line": "Montana Central: Great Falls \u2013 Helena", "id": "montana-central-great-falls-helena", "color": "#7B5EA7", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 30, "speed_source": "estimated: an 1880s mountain line worked with 4-4-0s; unsourced", "geometry_quality": "Real-traced. 152.1 km against a documented Great Falls\u2013Helena distance of about 96 miles (154 km), a 0.99x match. Largest straight-line bridge between OSM fragments 0.43 km. Great Falls endpoint snapped 0.96 km from the OSM place node, Helena 1.27 km. Drawn on the surviving alignment, which is the same one the Montana Central built.", "segments": [{"start": 1887, "end": 1890, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The Montana Central was incorporated Jan 25 1886 by James J. Hill's St. Paul, Minneapolis and Manitoba Railway, and its line from Great Falls to Helena was completed in November 1887, a month after the Manitoba road reached Great Falls from the east. Drawn only to 1890, when Hill transferred the Montana Central to the newly renamed Great Northern on Feb 1 of that year -- the post-1890 years are drawn as the Great Northern feature, in Great Northern colours (CLAUDE.md Rule 4). Frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["Segment-level: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montana_Central_Railway (Montana Central incorporated Jan 25 1886; Great Falls-Helena completed November 1887; ownership transferred to the Great Northern Feb 1 1890). Headway and speed are estimates.", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montana_Central_Railway"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-111.30342, 47.5011], [-111.30412, 47.49454], [-111.30091, 47.49431], [-111.31781, 47.49293], [-111.32329, 47.49195], [-111.34922, 47.48299], [-111.35488, 47.47958], [-111.36174, 47.47304], [-111.36906, 47.46935], [-111.48698, 47.43924], [-111.50693, 47.43462], [-111.50482, 47.43106], [-111.5123, 47.42851], [-111.51459, 47.42697], [-111.54558, 47.39558], [-111.57275, 47.36236], [-111.578, 47.35752], [-111.59621, 47.34332], [-111.59813, 47.34275], [-111.60394, 47.34253], [-111.60756, 47.34153], [-111.67814, 47.29551], [-111.68943, 47.28481], [-111.69681, 47.27568], [-111.69947, 47.27002], [-111.708, 47.25614], [-111.71156, 47.2454], [-111.71505, 47.24397], [-111.72094, 47.24419], [-111.75953, 47.22878], [-111.76411, 47.22766], [-111.76874, 47.22474], [-111.7723, 47.21945], [-111.77597, 47.20776], [-111.77857, 47.20468], [-111.79061, 47.1967], [-111.80053, 47.19238], [-111.81144, 47.17877], [-111.81833, 47.17739], [-111.81983, 47.17392], [-111.82191, 47.17228], [-111.82507, 47.17171], [-111.83032, 47.17252], [-111.83382, 47.16977], [-111.83551, 47.16549], [-111.83399, 47.16284], [-111.83139, 47.16049], [-111.83058, 47.15773], [-111.83123, 47.1565]